commit 0a85200733ef1f32849c682936c53c22fdff3226
Author: Jarod Castillo <43973221+Smotto@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Wed Nov 4 16:24:51 2026 +0000

    psi: check reach rule on termination promises and spell helper rankings (#1825)
    
    * psi: accept field rank subjects on recursive machines
    
    A recursive machine whose `terminates by` names a field of its record
    parameter was refused even when the field visibly counted down:
    
        machine iterate(pending: Pair) -> u32
        terminates by pending.left in 0..=5 {
            transition pending.left > 0 {
                true -> iterate(Pair { left: pending.left - 1, right: pending.right })
                false -> pending.left
            }
        }
    
    The checker reported "cannot prove rank range `0..=5`" (or, without the
    range, "cannot prove the `terminates by` ranking"). It now accepts this
    helper, nested or top-level, with the range bound taken from the data
    domain `Pair requires left <= 5`. A call that passes `left: pending.left`
    unchanged is still refused, now with the precise "cannot prove strict rank
    decrease on a recurrent arrival" diagnostic.
    
    Two steps failed. First, the arrival check could not read the new value
    of `pending.left` from the argument `Pair { ... }`. Building an owned
    record publishes a fresh value, so that later checks never re-derive the
    record's validity from equal fields. The completed-value observer
    projected fields from that fresh value, found no constructor, and returned
    no source for `left`. It now first recovers the constructor this exact
    occurrence evaluated (the judge's completed nominal receipt) and projects
    the field from it. Validity still stays on the fresh value; only the field
    description uses the receipt.
    
    Second, the call-cycle judgment rejected the accepted body with "a member
    lacks a supported exact ranking witness". Source lowering elaborates the
    default order of a plain `terminates by value` only for a bare formal, so
    a projected subject keeps a pending view. The checker already infers
    descending naturals from an unsigned carrier; the component judgment now
    reads the same default for a projected subject with an unsigned integer
    carrier.
    
    Validation: the new positive
    tests/omega/pass/termination/nested_helper_field_rank_subject_exit checks
    and runs with native and Terminal Psi exit 0 (`omega run --both`); its
    twin tests/omega/fail/termination/nested_helper_field_rank_subject_not_decreasing
    rejects with the strict-decrease diagnostic. The corpus gate over
    termination/rank_arrival, termination/rank_range and the two new fixtures
    (33 fixtures) moved only the two new rows; rank_range_state_call timed out
    under load, and takes 2m36s with both the base and the candidate binary
    alone. The 219 fixtures whose witness names a projected subject gave the
    same check outcome with base and candidate binaries except the new
    positive, and the 30 rank_arrival/rank_range fixtures gave identical
    diagnostics.
    
    * psi: bind the field rank witness on all hosted targets and record the frontier
    
    The new nested-helper field-rank positive now binds its entry for all
    four hosted targets. The termination board names the remaining ladder:
    component ranking still lacks captured-copy, formal/result-equation,
    opaque-child, range and stored-reference rank subjects, and the reach
    rule that rejects promises reaching unranked recursion lands after them.
    
    * psi: recover nested owned constructions behind field rank arrivals
    
    A recursive helper ranked by a nested field (terminates by
    pending.held.remaining) was refused with "cannot prove rank range", because
    the arrival built Bundle { held: Countdown { .. } } and each owned
    construction publishes its own fresh value. Rung 1 recovered only the outer
    constructor, so the projection found no constructor under held.
    
    Recover the evaluated constructor at every owned child on the path, each by
    its own capture identity. Work in progress: witnesses and validation follow.
    
    * psi: pin the nested field rank witness and its non-decreasing twin
    
    * psi: record the nested field rank rows and narrow the termination frontier
    
    * psi: prove record measure rank arrivals on single-member components
    
    A machine that ranked itself with a declared measure over a record, and
    recursed through a freshly built record, was refused with "cannot prove
    rank range". For example `walk(countdown: Countdown)` ranked by
    `countdown -> Countdown::Remaining in 0..=5` and calling
    `walk(Countdown { remaining: countdown.remaining - 1 })` was rejected,
    although the measure plainly drops by one on every call. The same
    refusal hit nested helpers ranked by `pending.held -> Countdown::Remaining`.
    These programs now check, and a call that rebuilds the record without
    lowering the measure is still rejected as not decreasing.
    
    A component with one authored member is judged by the relational edge
    proof, which works over integer polynomials: the source rank is an
    expression over the entry formals, and the arriving rank substitutes
    each formal with the call's actual. When declared measures moved to a
    declaration application with typed record inputs (#1113), that proof
    was not ported. It still tried to normalize the record subject as an
    integer, which has no meaning, so every recursive edge failed before
    range or descent was even considered.
    
    The edge proof now applies the declaration to records directly
    (`ranking_range/record_ranks.rs`). Each declared read of the measure
    names an exact field coordinate of the subject. Those coordinates join
    the edge's field set, so the guard `countdown.remaining > 0`, the data
    invariant `remaining <= 5` and the arrival substitution all read the
    same atoms the application consumes. The source rank applies the
    measure to those reads and the formal's entry snapshot. The arriving
    rank applies it again to the record this call builds: each read is the
    completed scalar of that call's own argument, and the whole-value
    snapshot is captured at that call's argument cut. The source snapshot
    never stands in for the arrival, so an equal constructor elsewhere
    supplies nothing. Range membership at both ends and strict decrease are
    then proved from the two formed applications. Reads that pass through a
    nested call inside the measure body are still refused on this path.
    
    Witnesses:
    - pass/termination/measure_field_rank_range checks again (base:
      "cannot prove rank range `0..=5`").
    - New pass/termination/nested_helper_record_measure_rank_exit: a nested
      helper ranked by `pending.held -> Countdown::Remaining`; `omega run
      --both` gives native exit 0 and interpreter exit 0 (base: rejected
      with the same rank-range refusal).
    - New fail/termination/nested_helper_record_measure_rank_not_decreasing:
      the same program passing `remaining: pending.held.remaining` rejects
      with "cannot prove strict rank decrease on a recurrent arrival".
    
    Validation: corpus gate on 186 fixtures (every fixture whose ranked
    measure takes a record formal, excluding pass rows already checked;
    the rank_arrival/rank_range fixtures that use such a measure; the new
    rows). 14 pass fixtures moved from rejected to checked; the remaining
    moves (29 joint-rank fail fixtures missing their fragment, 3 fail
    fixtures accepting, one package-hash diagnostic) reproduce identically
    with the base compiler (b3af558aea) run alone. validation and
    typed-trees-to-checked-trees tests pass (130), clippy -D warnings and
    rustfmt are clean.
    
    * psi: narrow the termination frontier after record measure arrivals
    
    * psi: record the record measure rank rows
    
    * psi: prove record measure arrivals through completed result equations
    
    A nested helper ranked by a declared measure over a record was refused
    with "cannot prove rank range" when its recursive call passed the record
    through another call, even when that callee promised to keep the ranked
    field. In the new fixture, `iterate(held: Countdown, ..)` is ranked by
    `held -> Countdown::Remaining in 0..=5` and recurses with
    `identity(Countdown { remaining: held.remaining - 1 })`, where `identity`
    declares `ensures result.remaining == value.remaining`. The measure
    plainly drops by one, and the program now checks and runs. Removing the
    `ensures`, or passing `remaining: held.remaining`, is still rejected.
    
    The record-measure edge proof forms the arriving rank by reading each
    declared field of the record the call passes. For a call result, that
    read asks the completed value of `identity(..)` for its `remaining`
    field. The call's completed value is its description (`identity`
    applied to its captured argument), and the callee's public equation is
    recorded about the call's captured result instead. Nothing connected the
    two, so the field had no value and the arrival failed to form.
    
    The structural judge already records a completed-call identity linking
    each captured result to its description. The field read now follows
    that identity back to its single captured occurrence, applies only the
    callee's public result equations to the projected field (the existing
    `completed_integer_call_description` view, which excludes call
    identities and never reads a body), and then reads the named actual
    through that occurrence's construction receipt. Here that gives
    `held.remaining - 1`. A field the contract does not mention, an
    ambiguous description shared by distinct captures, or a body without a
    contract supplies no read, and the edge still fails closed. Only the
    ranking consumers of completed-value observations use this read.
    
    Witnesses:
    - New pass/termination/nested_helper_record_measure_result_equation_exit:
      `omega run --both` gives native exit 0 and interpreter exit 0 (before:
      "cannot prove rank range `0..=5` for the rank produced by the selected
      view").
    - New fail/termination/nested_helper_record_measure_result_equation_missing
      (no `ensures`) rejects with that same rank-range refusal: without the
      public equation the returned field has no value, so the arriving rank
      cannot be formed.
    - New fail/termination/nested_helper_record_measure_result_equation_not_decreasing
      rejects with "cannot prove strict rank decrease on a recurrent
      arrival" (before: the rank-range refusal).
    - fail/termination/rank_arrival_missing_public_result_field_equation and
      rank_arrival_public_result_wrong_subject are unchanged: their helpers
      are unranked and still check.
    
    Validation: corpus gate on 147 fixtures (every corpus fixture with a
    ranked machine and a record-returning callee with a `result.field`
    contract, the two rank_arrival controls and the new rows). Apart from
    the new rows, all 51 structural moves reproduce identically with the
    pre-change compiler run alone, and the one slowdown measures the same
    with both. validation and typed-trees-to-checked-trees tests pass (130);
    clippy -D warnings, rustfmt and check_rules are clean.
    
    * psi: pin a result equation that names the wrong subject on a ranked helper
    
    * psi: record the result equation rank rows
    
    * psi: check reach rule on termination promises and spell helper rankings
    
    * psi: admit recursive inductive values as reference free and bound projected measures
    
    * tests: record outcomes for termination reach rule migration
    
